WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.700
Thanks to the Great Courses Plus for sponsoring
this episode.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:07.560
Hey Crazies.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.059
You’ve been asking for a good solid video
on black holes for a while

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:13.780
and there’s a reason I waited until
now to do it.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.700
I had to cover some relativity stuff first
or this video wouldn't have made any sense.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.140
Anyway, here we go!

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.840
It seems pretty natural to start this at the
beginning.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.100
No no! Too far!

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.520
Not the beginning of the universe, the beginning
of a black hole.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.900
A black hole forms when you get enough mass
into a small enough volume

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.160
and the best mechanism the universe has for
that is gravity.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.420
Say you’ve got a star with a sufficiently
large mass.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:41.079
Anything greater than 20 times the mass of
our Sun ought to do it.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:44.240
Now, not all of that mass is going to form
a black hole.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:49.040
Only the stellar core will, which is only
about 3 times the mass of our Sun.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.340
The other 17 solar masses are blasted into space by a supernova.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.220
At the end of a large star’s life, the core
is mostly made of iron,

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.739
but it doesn’t stay iron for much longer.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:03.880
The core’s own gravity collapses it into
a neutron star.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.380
That’s a ball of neutrons about the size
of a small city.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:11.960
Neutrons are capable of exerting some outward
pressure thanks to quantum mechanics,

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:15.620
but this thing is just too massive for it
to do any good.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.939
The neutron star continues to collapse under
its own gravity.

00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:23.090
Eventually, the mass is compressed into such
a small volume that

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:27.130
the space-time around the neutron star is
curved to an extreme.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:31.909
A perfectly-black bubble forms around it called
an event horizon.

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:34.020
This is a black hole.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.180
Except, it’s not actually a hole.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.560
This graphic is not what a black hole looks
like.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:40.729
Ignore these.

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:42.680
A black hole is a sphere.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:43.940
It’s a ball.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:47.860
But it’s so black, you can’t tell that
it’s three dimensional.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.980
It just looks like a circle from all directions.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:55.380
Black holes have been a part of general relativity
since it was published in 1915,

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.500
but the term “black hole” wasn't coined until the mid-1960s.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:03.420
Black ball isn’t nearly as catchy, exciting,
or scary.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.060
I mean, tell me what you think:

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.980
Black balls in space!

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.000
vs

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.620
Black holes in space!

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:14.260
Look, I’m not saying black holes aren’t
scary.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.400
Because they are.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.660
They’re terrifying!

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:19.840
But only because they let you get closer than
usual.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.300
Once those outer layers of the star get blasted
away, the collapse

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:28.460
of the core has no effect on the gravity outside
the original core’s volume.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:31.700
If anything is still orbiting, it will keep
orbiting.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.680
Nothing is going to suddenly get sucked in
just because it’s a black hole now.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.920
Black holes don’t suck!

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.780
All the weirdness that happens around a black
hole

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:44.100
happens inside the volume of the original
core.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:48.940
As the core collapses, it makes more space
accessible around itself.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.310
You can get a lot closer to that mass than
you could before.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:55.740
Now, before we move forward, we’re going
to make a big assumption.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:00.320
For simplicity, we’re going to assume this
black hole is not rotating.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.680
Yes, I know that’s not very realistic.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.640
But rotating black holes really deserve their
own video.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.480
Making that assumption, the first thing you’re
going to notice

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.520
is a sphere of orbiting light, which we naturally
call a photon sphere.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:14.860
Yeah, you heard me right!

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:15.870
Light.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:20.049
The thing that always travels the straightest
path possible through space-time

00:03:20.049 --> 00:03:22.390
can orbit the black hole at this place.

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:27.340
The diameter of this sphere is 1.5 times the
diameter of the event horizon.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:31.500
That’s about 16.5 miles across for our 3
solar-mass black hole.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:34.920
Outside that sphere, stable orbits are possible.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.560
Inside, orbits are destined to collapse into
the black hole.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:43.200
The next thing you’ll notice is that perfectly-black
and terrifying event horizon.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.960
Let’s talk a little more about that.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.700
The size of the event horizon is called the
Schwarzschild radius.

00:03:48.709 --> 00:03:52.549
For our 3 solar-mass black hole, it’s about
5.5 miles.

00:03:52.549 --> 00:03:57.909
Meaning it’s about 11 miles across,
which is a very small space to have 3 solar-masses

00:03:57.909 --> 00:03:59.040
crammed into.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.040
And, as far as general relativity is concerned,
this is the lower limit for the size of a

00:04:03.049 --> 00:04:04.050
black hole.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:08.390
The more mass you cram into a black hole,
the bigger the event horizon.

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:10.700
Black holes grow as they eat.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.420
Why do they call it a horizon?

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.440
Excellent question! It's actually a very good name.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.700
A horizon is a boundary we can’t see beyond.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:21.540
Kind of like the horizon when you stand on
the Earth.

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:25.039
The curvature of the Earth has hidden those
places from view.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.340
Likewise, an event horizon is a boundary hiding
events from view.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.080
As in space-time events.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.340
Yes, the same kind of events we’ve been
talking about in these relativity videos.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.900
To the space-time diagram!

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:41.420
Right before the neutron star turns into a
black hole, it’s moving forward in time.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:44.280
But these axes don’t belong to that neutron
star.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.620
They belong to a distant observer, like maybe
us on or near the Earth.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:53.040
To form a black hole, the neutron star must
collapse inside this boundary.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.720
A boundary that will eventually be the event
horizon, but isn’t yet.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.039
Speaking of which, once that event horizon
forms, we call that

00:05:02.039 --> 00:05:06.440
the outside edge of black hole instead of the
matter still collapsing behind it.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.440
Here’s why.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:11.540
As the last bit of neutron star collapses
inside the boundary,

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:13.640
the event horizon comes into being.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:17.860
Everything inside it is immediately separated
from the rest of the universe.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:20.800
It’s no longer part of the normal flow of
time.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.940
Once the event horizon forms, none of the
"now" that happens inside it

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:27.220
will ever be part of our "now."

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:32.660
A black hole is what it looks like when events
have been completely severed from the universe.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:34.980
So, in a way, it's kind of like a hole.

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:37.600
It’s a piece of the universe that’s missing.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:38.860
So what’s a black hole?

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:43.200
A black hole is a black spherical object with
the mass of at least 3 Suns.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:47.580
That mass is so compressed and the space-time
around it so curved

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:52.800
that all events inside its horizon are forever
separated from the universe.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.040
A black hole is a missing piece of space-time.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.220
So, got any questions about black holes?

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:01.540
Please ask in the comments.

